{"candidates":[{"claim":"The stall at 23 is best explained by a border-rank gap: approximate rank-22 decompositions of <3,3,3> exist (ALS converges to a small but strictly positive residual), but no exact rank-22 decomposition exists because the tensor's rank exceeds its border rank, placing it outside the rank-22 variety closure. Numerical methods thus perpetually approach but never close the gap, while exact symbolic/SAT searches fail because the target point is not in the variety at all.","counterconditions":["A sandbox-verified exact rank-22 decomposition of <3,3,3> is produced (directly refutes the nonexistence claim).","ALS on rank-22 for <3,3,3> converges to a residual within machine-epsilon of zero, suggesting exact rank-22 exists and the stall is a search-coverage problem, not a border-rank gap.","The border rank of <3,3,3> is proven equal to its rank (eliminates the gap mechanism entirely).","A rank-22 decomposition is found modulo a small prime p but fails to lift to characteristic zero, which would instead support a modular obstruction hypothesis rather than a border-rank gap."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"695e3101e0b7bef8","quote":"1. **Symmetry obstruction.** The `<3,3,3>` tensor has a large symmetry group. If every rank-22 decomposition would have to break that symmetry in a way that for"},{"block":"1db0851ec995fd3d","quote":"**R(3,3,3) is unknown.** The best construction anyone has found uses 23 multiplications. The best proved lower bound is 19. Whether 22, 21, or 20 is achievable "},{"block":"70df46c005c36307","quote":"23 has stood since 1976 despite: decades of hand construction; numerical alternating-least-squares searches; SAT and Groebner-basis attacks; and a"},{"block":"46903c7d73586c1f","quote":"Sanity anchor: Strassen's 2x2 algorithm is the r=7 case of exactly this format and can be verified by the same routine over the 64 entries of the `<2,2,2>` tens"}],"mechanism":"The rank-22 variety in (F^9 ⊗ F^9 ⊗ F^9) is an algebraic variety whose closure contains tensors of border rank ≤ 22. If border_rank(<3,3,3>) < rank(<3,3,3>), then <3,3,3> lies in the closure of the rank-22 variety but not in the variety itself. ALS (alternating least squares) minimizes the Frobenius residual ||<3,3,3> - Σ_k u_k⊗v_k⊗w_k|| and will converge toward a boundary point with positive residual, explaining decades of near-misses. SAT/Groebner searches encode exact membership in the variety, which is false, explaining their failure. The symmetry group of <3,3,3> (order ≥ 6·|S_3|·|GL_3| automorphisms per block [695e3101e0b7bef8]) further constrains the boundary geometry, potentially forcing the nearest boundary point into a high-symmetry orbit that ALS struggles to identify. A sandbox simulation can discriminate this from competing hypotheses (e.g., 'rank-22 exists but ALS is trapped in local minima') by checking whether the ALS residual for rank-22 on <3,3,3> stabilizes at a strictly positive plateau versus oscillating, and by calibrating the verifier on Strassen's rank-7 <2,2,2> where the residual must reach exactly zero.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-3d0149887933712bd39b94aed8b8f843ca3fb9e6"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72}],"simulation_proposals":[]}